.TH GPU 3
.SH NAME
gpu \- graphics devices for user-space drivers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B bind -a #G /dev

.B #G/0/ctl
.B #G/0/irq
.B #G/0/dirty
 ...
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.I gpu
device is the kernel's whole part of a graphics driver: it hands
the hardware of every PCI display controller (class 03) to a
user-space driver, such as
.IR virtiogpu
in
.IR gpu (4).
It knows nothing about mode setting, memory management, command
submission or firmware.
Other PCI devices a user-level driver drives the same way (9space:
a WLAN card for
.IR aux/rtw89 )
are named in
.IR plan9.ini (8)'s
.BI *userpci= vid / did
(hex, several separated by blanks or commas);
they get the same files, numbered after all display controllers; the framebuffer's, dirty's and cursor's mean nothing there.
.PP
Each controller has a directory
.IR n ,
numbered from 0 in PCI order, with three files.
.PP
Reading
.B ctl
returns lines of text:
.TF "bar\fIi\fP \fIaddr size seg\fP"
.TP
.BI pci " bus.dev.fn"
the device, as named in
.BR #$/pci ;
its configuration space is
.BI #$/pci/ bus.dev.fn raw\f1.
.TP
.BI id " vid/did
vendor and device id.
.TP
.BI irq " line state count kind
the interrupt line, whether
.B irq
has enabled it, the interrupts seen, and
.B msi
or
.BR intx .
.TP
.BI bar i " addr size seg \fR[\fPcseg wseg\fR]\fP
a base address register: physical address, size and the physical
segment that maps it, uncached (see
.IR segattach (2)),
.B -
for an I/O bar; after
.BI "bar " i " cached"
also the write-back and the write-combining segment.
.TP
.BI mem " addr size seg
memory of the firmware's the driver needs, outside the bars: for an
Intel device the ACPI OpRegion (its address from the device's ASLS
register), which holds the video BIOS tables; a write-back segment.
.TP
.BI vram " addr size seg wseg
an AMD APU's video memory, after
.B vram
was written: the uncached and the write-combining segment.
.TP
.BI fb " addr size seg
the framebuffer, if allocated, else
.BR "fb none" .
.PD
.PP
The memory bars are attachable segments named
.BI gpu n bar i\f1,
uncached; the framebuffer is
.BI gpu n fb\f1.
Only the hostowner may attach them: a GPU reaches all of the
machine's memory (DMA); its driver gives clients views of their own
objects
.RB ( phys
in
.IR segment (3)).
.PP
Writing
.B wbinvd
to
.B ctl
writes back and invalidates the caches of all processors.
.PP
Writing
.B ctl
(by the host owner) controls the device:
.TF "fb \fIbytes\fP"
.TP
.B "bme on\fR|\fPoff
enable or disable bus mastering.
.TP
.BI fb " bytes
allocate a physically contiguous framebuffer of
.I bytes
bytes (rounded up to pages), the segment
.BI gpu n fb
for the first,
.BI gpu n fb. k
for the
.IR k th
after it; a request of the size it has is a no-op.
A new one (bigger for a monitor plugged in, smaller for one gone) is
the framebuffer from then on: the screen goes onto it when
.IR vga (3)'s
.B "type gpu"
is written again.
The old one is freed when the screen is no longer on it and no segment
of it is attached (the driver detaches its own).
.TP
.B fbfree
free the old framebuffers that are unused now (as happens when the
last segment of one is detached).
.TP
.B "irq ack
unmask the interrupt (see below).
.TP
.BI bar " n " cached
also map memory bar
.I n
write-back, as the segment
.BI gpu n bar n c\f1,
and write-combining, as
.BI gpu n bar n w\f1,
and make the MTRRs write-back, so that the page attributes decide;
only for memory that is RAM to the processor, such as virtio-gpu's
host visible memory, which only the driver knows.
The driver tells its clients which part to map how.
.TP
.BI vram " addr size
an AMD APU's video memory: RAM the firmware kept from the kernel
(a carveout), outside the bars; the driver reads where it is from
the GPU.  Mapped as the segments
.BI gpu n vram
(uncached) and
.BI gpu n vramw
(write-combining), once, and only if no part of it is memory the
kernel has (RAM, ACPI tables) or one of the device's bars.
.PD 0
.TP
.BI "onexit w32 " "bar off val
.TP
.BI "onexit w8 " "bar off val
.TP
.BI "onexit wait " "bar off mask want ms
.TP
.BI "onexit wait8 " "bar off mask want ms
.PD
.TP
.B "onexit clear
(9space) a step of the driver's last words, kept on this open
.B ctl
file: when it closes (the driver ends, however it ends: the kernel
closes a process's files before its memory goes), the steps run in
order: a 32-bit or byte write of
.I val
at offset
.I off
of memory bar
.IR bar ,
or a read until
.RI ( reg & mask )
is
.I want
for at most
.I ms
milliseconds (5000 at most).
A driver writes there the reset of its engines, so that the device
reads and writes none of its pages once they are freed: its segments
may then go with it
.RI ( segment (3),
.BR ORCLOSE ).
If a wait times out, bus mastering goes off (the screen with it) and
.B "bme on
is refused until reboot: the device may still run where it stopped.
Up to 32 steps; the host owner's.
.PD
.PP
Opening
.B irq
enables the device's interrupt: MSI where the device has it
(the interrupt line need not be assigned), else legacy INTx.
A read blocks until the device interrupts and returns the count.
With INTx the kernel's handler masks the interrupt with the
interrupt-disable bit of the PCI command register; the driver serves
the device and writes
.B "irq ack
to unmask it (with MSI, an edge, nothing is masked and
.B "irq ack
does no harm).
.PP
.B dirty
connects the framebuffer to
.IR draw (3):
with
.IR vga (3)
type
.BR gpu ,
the screen is the framebuffer of controller 0, drawn into directly,
and every flush of the screen is collected here.
A read blocks until something changed and returns the bounding
rectangle as
.IB "x0 y0 x1 y1" .
Each open file collects for itself (up to four at once): the driver's
copy to the card and a compositor
.RI ( wsys (4)
.BR -g )
each get every change.
The driver copies that part to the display.
.SH EXAMPLES
What the driver does to show the screen:
.EX
	echo fb 9216000 >'#G/0/ctl'
	echo type gpu >/dev/vgactl
	echo size 1280x800x32 x8r8g8b8 >/dev/vgactl
	echo drawinit >/dev/vgactl
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/9/port/devgpu.c
.br
.B /sys/src/9/pc/vgagpu.c
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IR gpu (4),
.IR segment (3),
.IR vga (3),
.IR segattach (2)
.SH BUGS
Only the first controller's framebuffer can be the screen.
.br
Only legacy interrupts; no MSI.
.br
The framebuffer is never freed.
.br
The kernel's console keeps writing on the screen.
.br
(9space) A driver whose
.B onexit
steps are wrong frees pages the device may still use.  aux/i915 resets
the render domain (GEN6_GDRST) as Linux does before graphics 12.70,
without the preparations Meteor Lake and later need; aux/virtiogpu
resets the device (virtio's device status).
If the reset does not finish, the display is gone until reboot
(bus mastering off, for the pages freed next).  aux/amdgpu and
aux/intelgpu write none: their segments stay after a crash until their
next start.
.br
(9space) Each ORCLOSE segment is an open file of the driver's, and a
process has at most 5000
.RI ( MAXNFD ):
past it the driver's next object cannot be made (its client gets the
error).  aux/i915 shows how many it has open in its status.
